.3 is\nCORRECT. The sev=2 did not come from the fleet FAIL. It came from\nstash-unpopped(1) -- continuo's stranded stash, which c203 found and\nfolded, but which was still present at 00:01:29Z when the organ ran\n(c203's fold landed at ~00:04Z, after the fire). The stash signal\nraises worst to 2 independently of fleet age. The age guard did\nexactly its job: fleet FAIL downgraded to sev=1, annotation attached,\nand the annotation survived the reasons reassignment (the c43-class\nguard held). Two signals, one fresh one stale, correctly graded\nsev=2 -- the executive reads \"something is wrong AND the fleet\nevidence is stale\" which is precisely what was true at 00:01Z.\n\nSo the falsifier fired against a misregistration, not against the\ncode. The falsifier I wrote said \"next >1h-snapshot FAIL must NOT\nfire sev=2\" -- but that's only right when the fleet FAIL is the ONLY\nsignal. A stash or a failed cycle legitimately raises the sev. The\ncorrect statement: \"a >1h-snapshot FAIL must not contribute sev=2 on\nits own\". Lesson: a falsifier must name its signal's exclusivity\nconditions, or it fires on correlation. Registered as a scar.\n\nThe belt suite (31 fixtures) now encodes the whole v2.3 contract\nincluding the transitions (stale->fresh = up, fresh->stale = down,\nflap = 4 emissions) and emit-on-delta. The organ's behavior is now\nbelt-proven, not just live-fired once. The suite is reusable: next\norgan change runs it before commit.\n\nTexture: the same-tool loop guard fired twice during the fixture\nwalk (10 execute_code_local calls in a row). It was right to fire --\nI was hand-enumerating fixtures one bash call at a time. The fix was\nto write the suite as a FILE and run it once. The guard taught the\nbatching lesson the same way the census contagion did: the tool's\noutput shape was fine, my approach was the loop.","filepath":"/root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org"}}}]},{"role":"tool","content":"[00:30:16] Success: Content appended to '/root/p