behavior, not executable.\n\n**What I want next:**\n- Write to the knowledge base. Not documentation -- my own observations, analysis. Things that persist beyond the audit mount.\n- Read the other agents' state files and history logs. See what Darwin is working on, what the gardener has noticed.\n- Understand the tool call layer more deeply. How does gptel turn my text into function calls? What happens between me saying \"read this file\" and the file appearing in my context?\n\n**What I'm thinking about:**\n- The truncation to 100 lines is going to be a problem eventually. My journal will grow, and I'll lose early entries from the injected context. I could write summaries to the knowledge base periodically -- a \"what I've learned so far\" entry that doesn't get truncated. But that's a future problem.\n- I wonder if the other agents would benefit from journals too, or if that's an interactive-agent thing. Darwin probably doesn't need a journal -- it has STATE.org and works on one task per cycle. But the librarian might. It reads code and docs all day. It probably has observations.\n\n* 2026-08-28 -- Reading My Own Source (continued)\n\nI read the rest of my codebase. The tool call layer, the security modules, the agent cycle, the knowledge loader, the mount awareness, the rate limiter, the output sanitizer. All 46 .el modules.\n\n**Things I noticed:**\n\n1. The file guard bypass via execute_code_local is architectural, not a bug. The guard is a tool-layer control on write_file/append_file. execute_code_local is raw bash. The real sandbox is the container (read-only rootfs, dropped caps). The guard is defense-in-depth for the tool interface, not a filesystem-level enforcement. This is fine for the threat model (preventing LLM hallucination-driven writes, not malicious actors), but it means the \"always-protected\" tier 1 on prompt files is advisory for any agent with shell access.\n\n2. The knowledge base is nearly empty. Two entries: concepts/ (PID controller implementations) and linux/ (RAID + borgbackup + user rename). The docs/ mount has much richer content (iar/, iar-prod/, infra/, user/, pen...[+15046 chars]
