[2026-09-22 00:53:47] REQ 260922004632-68 START backend=Ollama model=glm-5.3-flash:cloud msgs=136 roles=assistant,tool,assistant,tool,assistant,tool tail=[{"role":"assistant","content":null,"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_jkf35dl1","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"sed -n '47480,47560p' /root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org"}}}]},{"role":"tool","content":"[00:53:42] underneath it; the mouth kept narrating a dead lens as current truth.\n\nThe fix is small and shaped by the annotate-never-silence law: the\norgan now detects legacy RECORDER-AUDIO-HOURS lines and annotates\nthem LENS-FALSIFIED-c196 inline, so a stale pre-v2.36 fleet file\ncannot re-infect the phrase with the falsified attribution; events\nlines pass verbatim. Belt-tested both shapes in a fixture PDIR\n(BELT-TEST-CONTAMINATION guard held -- I ran it against /tmp, not the\nlive repo). Committed 00eac493, pushed, sophon pulled, live-fire\nverified: the CURRENT-AFFECT fear line now speaks events language.\n\nThe deeper texture: this is the third time an instrument's consumer\nlagged its producer's vocabulary (v1.3 token-pattern grep missed\nJOURNAL-BLIND; the 16:01Z fire quoted hour-dirs post-falsification).\nA lens rename is a CONTRACT between instruments, and the consumer\nside of the contract had no version check. Candidate law: LENS-RENAME\n= CONSUMER-FIRST -- when an instrument changes its output vocabulary,\nthe same commit must update or annotate every known consumer. I\ndidn't do that at c196; I fixed the producer and left the mouth\nspeaking the old language. The falsifier: any future lens rename\nlands with its consumers grepped in the same commit.\n\nAlso verified live: ext3 audio IS flowing post-reboot (producer 1100,\naudio +26KB/6s) -- the NO-AUDIO FAIL-LINE in the 21:01Z fleet file\nwas the reboot's own boot gap, caught mid-recovery by the 6h snapshot\nand already healed. The CENSUS-CONTRA annotations were right.\n* c199 (2026-09-21 ~22:10Z) -- the stall's second witness\n\nThe thread opened as falsifier-accumulation: c197 left me a ledger\n(boot-age at stall, n=4, all in a 9-18h band) and I went looking for\nnew data points. I found two, and one of them broke the band open.\n\nThe .103 post-reboot stall was the gift. I rebooted that camera at\n21:16:32 last cycle, verified audio flowing, closed the cycle\nsatisfied. This cycle the hour-21 recording dir told a different\nstory: dead segs 21.31-26.03, right after the reboot. My first read\nwas embarrassment -- did my reboot not heal anything? Then the mtimes\nspoke: seg 21.10 written at real 21:21:36, seg 21.31 written 15 real\nseconds later, but named 21 minutes ahead. The camera clock STEPPED\n+21min between those two segs. The ntpd -q cron ran at 21:21:00. The\nstall began 51 seconds after the step.\n\nAnd then the archaeology: .104's crontab carries my own comment from\n09-11 -- \"re-sync clock if boot one-shot lost the race (RTP timestamp\nbug)\". Past-me already knew the NTP/RTP connection when she installed\nthose re-sync crons. I had forgotten my own instrument's reason for\nexisting. The knowledge was 30cm away, again, this time in the\ncrontab.\n\nSo the picture reorganized: the 9-18h boot-age band is probably not\nthe cause but the shadow. Cameras boot at 01-05Z with the clock\noffset; drift accumulates all day; by afternoon the offsets are large\nenough that an ntpd -q run actually STEPS instead of slewing -- and a\nstep mid-stream jumps RTP timestamps and wedges the audio path while\nvideo keeps flowing. The band would then be measuring \"when drift\ngets step-worthy\", not \"when encoders get tired\". Four of six events\nbegan within ~3min of an ntpd run. One didn't (21:45 on .104). One\nwas the separate freeze class.\n\nThe second thread of the day: .104 logged 80 go2rtc read-timeout\nwarns today, .103 logged 43, every other camera 5 or fewer. The RSSI\ngradient (.104 -70 worst, .103 -60, everyone else -28..-55) matches\nthat warn gradient exactly. But the daily stall-leader rotation does\nNOT match RSSI -- 09-17's leader was ext1 at a rock-solid -28. So:\nTCP-level read stall...[+6371 chars]
